[NAME]
buddydrive \- P2P encrypted folder backup for you and your buddies

[DESCRIPTION]
BuddyDrive backs up folders to a buddy across the internet, bypassing NATs and firewalls. No cloud. No middleman. Just peer-to-peer encrypted backup.
.PP
Each buddy keeps your folders encrypted in a storage folder of their own, so they are storage, not a co-author: they cannot read your file names or your data. The side that owns a folder is the only authority on moves, deletes and restores.
.PP
BuddyDrive consists of two main components:
.TP
.B buddydrive
The command-line interface and sync daemon. This is the core component that handles folder synchronization, peer discovery through the relay API, NAT traversal, and end-to-end encryption using XSalsa20-Poly1305.
.TP
.B buddydrive-gui
A GTK4 desktop application for monitoring and configuration. Provides a graphical interface to view sync status, manage folders and buddies, and configure settings.

[FILES]
.TP
.I ~/.buddydrive/config.toml
Configuration file
.TP
.I ~/.buddydrive/state.db
Runtime state database
.TP
.I ~/.buddydrive/index.db
File metadata index
.TP
.I ~/.buddydrive/storage/
A buddy's backed-up folders, one directory per buddy and folder id

[ENVIRONMENT]
.TP
.B BUDDYDRIVE_CONFIG_DIR
Directory holding config.toml (default ~/.buddydrive)
.TP
.B BUDDYDRIVE_DATA_DIR
Directory holding state.db, index.db, storage and logs (default: the config dir)

[EXAMPLES]
.TP
Initialize BuddyDrive:
buddydrive init
.TP
Add a folder to back up:
buddydrive add-folder ~/Documents --name docs
.TP
Generate a pairing code for a buddy:
buddydrive add-buddy --generate-code --id <buddy-id>
.TP
Start the sync daemon:
buddydrive start
.TP
Stop it again:
buddydrive stop
.TP
View configured state:
buddydrive status

[SEE ALSO]
buddydrive-gui(1)

